   "text": "I read the structure in three parts and joined them through the two amide linkages.\n\n- **Left part:** a 1H-indazole attached through its 6-position carbonyl to the nitrogen of a 5,7-dichloro-1,2,3,4-tetrahydroisoquinoline.\n- **Middle:** the tetrahydroisoquinoline carries Cl at C5 and C7, and a carboxamide at C6 that links to the amino acid.\n- **Right part:** the amide nitrogen is attached to a stereocentre bearing CO2H, with a CH2\u2013NH\u2013C(=O) chain leading to a piperidine. That piperidine nitrogen is part of a urea, and its 3-position carries an OH.\n\n**Stereochemistry:**\n- The \u03b1-carbon has a wedge to the CH2 group. The priorities are N > CO2H > CH2N. Tracing N \u2192 CO2H \u2192 CH2 gives counterclockwise with H at the back, so this carbon is S. In the SMILES this is `[C@@H]` when written as `N[C@@H](CN\u2026)C(=O)O`.\n- The piperidine C3 has a wedge to OH. The priorities are OH > C2 > C4. Tracing OH \u2192 C2 \u2192 C4 gives clockwise with H at the back, so this carbon is R. In the SMILES this is `[C@@H]` when written as `N3CCC[C@@H](O)C3`.\n\nSMILES: O=C(c1ccc2cn[nH]c2c1)N1CCc2c(Cl)c(C(=O)N[C@@H](CNC(=O)N3CCC[C@@H](O)C3)C(=O)O)c(Cl)cc2C1"
